00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.220 align:start position:0%
 
moving on former defense secretary  General James Madison studio 57 for his  first live interview since resigning  from the Trump administration his new  book is titled callsign chaos learning  to lead in it general mattis writes I  did as well as I could for as long as I  could when my concrete solutions and  strategic advice especially keeping  faith with our allies no longer  resonated it was time to resign Journal  mattis served as Secretary of Defense  for nearly two years during his long  career in the Marine Corps before his  service as defense secretary he held key  roles leading troops involved in the  wars in Iraq and Afghanistan general  good morning it's good to see you  you served almost two years I know you  expected to serve for how is this  unexpected summer going it's going  wonderfully west of the Rockies tell me  I'm enjoying life thing I don't want  just Washington I I miss the people in  Washington I don't miss any of the  issues Anthony  not one bit you told our David Martin  that the pullout from Syria announced  pullout by President Trump of troops  from Syria weighed heavily in your  decision to resign how did you know that  it was time not only on watching that  issue has reversed itself troops are now  remaining in Syria so I'm curious do you  wish you could reverse yourself and go  back no I decisions I don't have regrets  about them I've always believed strongly  that America needs allies I stand on  that because my formative experiences  were that whenever we were in trouble we  had allies with us and I wanted the  president to have someone more aligned  with his views so I I needed to leave it  what do you think the president got  wrong about Syria well I think when you  leave an administration over policy  matters and I laid that out in the  letter when I noted the need for allies  and all I think you all a period of  silence afterwards we have a million  troops around the world who are trying  to defend this experiment we call  America I don't believe that  administration officials should should  leave the administration and then start  creating controversy with comments  outside when they  they owe a period of Ivan call it a duty  of silence long enough for the Secretary  of State the president secretary of  defense to protect the country if not  controversial excuse me if not  controversy maybe compliments this is a  book about leadership and a good book at  that what would you say is your favorite  leadership quality of the sitting  president well I think we started right  in the book Bing West my co-author and I  in 2013 we were on version 5 in 2017  when I was called to join the  administration and it's not a book about  the sitting president so I'd prefer to  stay firmly focused on what good  leadership is like I I'd lead 40 sailors  and Marines in an infantry platoon I'd  led 250,000 soldiers sailors airmen  Marines Coast Guardsmen Allied troops  later you learn a lot about yourself you  learn a lot about leadership when you go  through that and I wanted to pass this  on when and why did you decide to  include your time with the Trump  administration into this book like you  said you've been writing this for five  years yes what I did Maria Elena was I  put into the prologue why the book had  been delayed for two years coming out  why it was coming out at this time and  my view is that government service is a  privilege it's also a duty I was brought  up that way and when any president  Republican or Democrat asks you to do  something as long as you're prepared to  do it you just roll up your sleeves and  your work you don't get into you know  wringing their hands and wondering about  it just pitch in and do the best you can  to help support the governance of the  country resignation later it's in that  book and it's been made public did was  it your decision to leave or were you  fired  were you pushed into resigning or  pressured into his thing it was  completely my decision to leave I had I  believe a good relationship with the  president I've read some of the stories  that we didn't have a good relationship  it was very forthright I would have time  with the president weekly over lunch  private lunch or in his office the  president knew everything I was doing  and I understood what the president  wanted done there just came a point  where I thought he needed a different  Secretary of Defense the president the  the day before you resigned last  December the president on Twitter said  we have won against Isis secretary  Pompeo was here a couple weeks ago and  he said in some places now Isis is more  powerful than it was three or four years  ago what do you think has happened the  challenge we face today is that  terrorism is an ambient threat it's  simply out there it's going to be there  on 9/11 we were reminded of that right  here in this town when over 3,000  innocent people were murdered I think  secretary Pompeo has it right and I  would leave those who are in the  administration now to speak to the  threat secretary of homeland security  secretary McAlinden told Anthony Mason  not long ago that the biggest threat  internally is white extremism white  supremacist extremism would you say that  there needs to be a reprioritizing of  defense money toward that threat here at  home I don't give policy advice from  what I call the cheap seats outside the  people in those seats right now have  that response rate however I would agree  that divisive nough since ID this  country is probably the biggest threat  the lack of respect the lack of  listening to one another and accepting  that even people who disagree with this  may be right once in a while you think  the president can heal that divisive  miss well I think that this is not about  one person or one administration this  goes back a while and it's time for all  at least the majority of us to roll up  our sleeves and get to work what's  printed on the coin that every one of us  had in our pocket the pluralist unum out  of many one there are many areas where I  think we have agreement we're no longer  talking about those and we need to talk  about common and ground as much as we  need to talk about where we disagree and  when you talk about this internal  divisive 'no set is the biggest threat  to our democracy are you talking about  congress or you're talking about the  politicians being divided are you  talking about the country being divided  what do you think it's a biggest threat  to our democracy Elena I think it's all  of those  I believe what has happened is when we  run elections it's always about dividing  I'm smart you're not I'm I'm wise and  you're you're dumb it's not always  pretty it's not always civil well  welcome to democracy you once the  elections over you go into governance  and it's not about divisive niche it's  about unity at that but we no longer  seem to go to governance which like  we're in a constant election mode and  constantly finding fault reason to cheer  against each other instead of working  together in the military you could not  win on the battlefield in a corporation  you could not win in the business place  with that sort of divisive miss inside  general mattis thank you so much for  being with us this morning callsign  chaos learning to lead goes on sale  tomorrow and

